A certificate carrying a critical extension nobody models (1.3.6.1.4.1.99999.1). RFC 5280 4.2 says
a verifier must refuse such a certificate, so x509Parse reports the OID in `critUnknown instead of
dropping it -- silently ignoring a critical extension is a way a verifier gets fooled.

openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -keyout /dev/null -noenc -sha256 \
  -not_before 20260101000000Z -not_after 20360101000000Z -set_serial 0x1234 \
  -subj "/CN=critical.neant.test" \
  -addext "basicConstraints=critical,CA:FALSE" \
  -addext "1.3.6.1.4.1.99999.1=critical,DER:0500" \
  -out tests/data/critical-unknown-ext.pem

